.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_PEER_CERT_VERIFIED 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified \- report whether the TLS client presented a verified certificate
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

bool chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns true only when three things hold: the request arrived over
TLS, the client presented a certificate in the handshake, and that
certificate verified against the ca_bundle_path of the server.

The result is false for a plaintext connection, for a server without
a ca_bundle_path, for a client that presented no certificate, and for
a NULL req.

In the default configuration a server with a ca_bundle_path accepts
no client that lacks such a certificate, so there every request
reports true.  With client_cert_optional set in chttp_tls_config_t a
client that presents nothing is accepted too, and a handler or a
middleware uses this function to tell that client apart from a
verified one.  In both modes a client whose certificate fails to
verify never reaches a handler.

The answer is a property of the connection.  Every request on one
keep-alive connection reports the same value, and a resumed TLS
session reports the answer of the handshake that set it up.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
true or false, as described above.
.fi
